.TH LS 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
ls \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
[\fB\-alhRtS\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIPATH\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
List information about the PATH(s) (the current directory by default). Sorts alphabetically unless \fB\-t\fR or \fB\-S\fR is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Do not hide entries starting with \fB.\fR
.TP
.B \-l
Use a long listing format (mode, links, owner, group, size, name).
.TP
.B \-h
With \fB\-l\fR, print sizes in human-readable form (e.g. 1.2K, 3.4M).
.TP
.B \-R
List subdirectories recursively.
.TP
.B \-t
Sort by modification time, newest first.
.TP
.B \-S
Sort by file size, largest first.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH NOTES
Owner/group names are resolved via the host's passwd/group database (common::users) -- see mitos-utils-integration(7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a general failure (a listed file could not be processed, etc.), 2 on a usage error (missing or invalid arguments).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
